1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,010
Now you will hear a speaker talking to a class on a trip to a marine aquarium.

2
00:00:04,010 --> 00:00:08,010
Hello, everyone. Thank you for visiting the aquarium today.

3
00:00:08,010 --> 00:00:11,340
I hope you've enjoyed seeing the thousands of fish we have here

4
00:00:11,340 --> 00:00:13,020
as well as the other sea animals.

5
00:00:13,020 --> 00:00:16,200
During the next hour we're going to show you some more sea creatures.

6
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:20,110
They all come from a body of water called the Sargasso Sea.

7
00:00:20,110 --> 00:00:23,190
The Sargasso Sea is actually part of the Atlantic Ocean.

8
00:00:23,190 --> 00:00:26,440
What I mean is---it isn't a separate body of water

9
00:00:27,060 --> 00:00:29,030
it's more like a sea within an ocean.

10
00:00:29,030 --> 00:00:33,280
It's located off the southern half of North America, and it is very large

11
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:35,140
it covers millions of kilometers.

12
00:00:35,140 --> 00:00:38,180
Now, a couple of things make it distinctive.

13
00:00:38,180 --> 00:00:44,060
First, and probably most important, the waters there are very calm-calm and warm.

14
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:46,080
There's also very little wind there.

15
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:53,030
Surrounding the Sargasso Sea are water currents in the Atlantic that move in a circular motion, counterclockwise.

16
00:00:53,030 --> 00:00:55,190
This water swirls around the Sargasso Sea.

17
00:00:55,190 --> 00:00:57,460
Because of the way these currents move

18
00:00:57,780 --> 00:01:01,070
the water in the middle of the Sargasso Sea doesn't move much at all.

19
00:01:01,070 --> 00:01:03,847
Because of the still waters and the lack of wind,

20
00:01:03,847 --> 00:01:07,300
sailing ships crossing the Atlantic travel much more slowly

21
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:08,290
when they get to the Sargasso Sea.

22
00:01:08,290 --> 00:01:12,270
Without wind, sailboats can get stuck there for long periods.

23
00:01:12,270 --> 00:01:18,080
Something else that's unusual about the Sargasso Sea is the seaweed floating on its surface.

24
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:23,020
Large amounts of a kind of seaweed called sargassum float on top of the water there.

25
00:01:23,020 --> 00:01:24,353
And because of the currents,

26
00:01:24,353 --> 00:01:26,660
the seaweed stays in the Sargasso Sea

27
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:29,150
it's kind of held in place by the rotating currents.

28
00:01:29,150 --> 00:01:32,462
Now this seaweed is what interests us most here at the aquarium,

29
00:01:32,462 --> 00:01:36,051
because it supports all kinds of sea life, like shrimp, crabs, and fish.

30
00:01:36,051 --> 00:01:38,536
Sargassum creates an ecosystem for them to live in.

31
00:01:38,536 --> 00:01:41,020
Sargassum creates an ecosystem for them to live in.

32
00:01:41,020 --> 00:01:47,475
What makes this ecosystem quite remarkable is that the creatures there are one you'd expect to find much closer to shore,

33
00:01:47,475 --> 00:01:51,290
much closer to land, not out in the middle of the Atlantic Ocean.

34
00:01:51,290 --> 00:01:56,270
Most likely, sargassum probably did not exist in the Sargasso originally.

35
00:01:56,270 --> 00:02:02,678
Rather, the seaweed and some of the ecosystem it supported drifted out into the Sargasso Sea long ago

36
00:02:02,678 --> 00:02:05,170
and became trapped there by the currents.

37
00:02:05,170 --> 00:02:09,090
From there, it simply spread all over the Sargasso Sea.
